{
  "title": "Parosteal Osteosarcoma",
  "url": "https://www.orthobullets.com/pathology/8015/parosteal-osteosarcoma",
  "subtitle": "Updated: Oct 22 2025",
  "sections": [
    {
      "text": "summary",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Parosteal Osteosarcoma is a malignant, low-grade, osteosarcoma that occurs on the surface of the metaphysis of long bones. Patients typically present between ages 30 and 40 with a painless mass.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Diagnosis is made radiographically with a heavily ossified, lobulated mass arising from the cortex with biopsy showing cellular atypia seen between regularly arranged osseous trabeculae.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Treatment is generally wide surgical resection.",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Epidemiology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Demographics",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "more common in females, age 30-40",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Anatomic location",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "occurs on **surface of metaphysis** of long bones",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "4",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "most common sites include **posterior distal femur**, proximal tibia, and proximal humerus",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": [
                {
                  "text": "80% cases occur in the femur",
                  "table": null,
                  "images": [],
                  "importance": "1",
                  "children": []
                }
              ]
            },
            {
              "text": "marrow invasion occurs in 25% of cases",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Symptoms",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Presentation",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "often presents as a **painless mass**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "limited joint motion can also be a presenting complaint for characteristic large posterior distal femoral lesions",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Imaging",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Radiographs",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "**heavily ossified, lobulated mass** arising from cortex",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8015/images/Case%20A%20-%20posterior%20femur%20-%20xray%20-%20Parsons_moved.png",
                  "caption": ""
                },
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8015/images/Case%20B%20-%20post%20femur%20-%20xray%20Ap%20-%20Parsons_moved.png",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "appears stuck onto cortex",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "CT chest",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mandatory staging study",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "used to evaluate for pulmonary metastasis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "MRI",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mandatory and must include entire involved bone",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "helps determine soft tissue/marrow involvement and skip lesions",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Bone scan",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "mandatory imaging study",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "always hot",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Histology",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Characteristic histology",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "low grade lesion",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "regularly arranged **normal osseous trabeculae**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8015/images/parosteal%20osteosarcoma%20histology.jpg",
                  "caption": "Low power histology slide showing classic parosteal osteosarcoma"
                },
                {
                  "src": "https://upload.orthobullets.com/topic/8015/images/Histology%20C_moved.jpg",
                  "caption": ""
                }
              ],
              "importance": "5",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "**most prominent histologic feature are the atypical spindle cells seen between regularly arranged osseous trabeculae**",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            },
            {
              "text": "some cellular atypia should be seen to make diagnosis",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "3",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Histology can be mistaken for **fibrous dysplasia**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "**High rate of MDM2 amplification and ring chromosome**",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Differential",
      "table": null,
      "images": [],
      "importance": "3",
      "children": [
        {
          "text": "Fibrous dysplasia",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "1",
          "children": [
            {
              "text": "similar on histology, but xrays are different",
              "table": null,
              "images": [],
              "importance": "1",
              "children": []
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "text": "Myositis Ossificans Traumatica",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Osteochondral exostosis (shares cortex with bone)",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "3",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "Developmental defect at insertion of adductor magnus",
          "table": null,
          "images": [],
          "importance": "4",
          "children": []
        },
        {
          "text": "",
